v4l2_codec2: keep decoding after the output surface is switched

Apps that show video in a TextureView switch the decoder's output surface
(MediaCodec.setOutputSurface) when the view moves, e.g. Reddit opening a
post from the feed. The surface-backed block pool then hands out new
buffers and the old ones never return. VideoFramePool dropped every buffer
beyond the V4L2 buffer count and V4L2Decoder had no V4L2 buffer for a new
block, so decoding stopped after a few frames: the player never became
ready and the video kept loading.

Surface-backed pools no longer drop unknown buffers (the surface bounds how
many are out), and a new block takes any free V4L2 buffer, whose old block
is forgotten.

diff --git a/components/VideoFramePool.cpp b/components/VideoFramePool.cpp
index d3fddb2..66e2bcd 100644
--- a/components/VideoFramePool.cpp
+++ b/components/VideoFramePool.cpp
@@ -151,7 +151,19 @@ bool VideoFramePool::shouldDropBuffer(uint32_t bufferId) {
         return false;
     }
 
-    return true;
+    // A surface-backed pool hands out new buffers after the client switches
+    // the output surface (MediaCodec.setOutputSurface, as apps do when a
+    // TextureView moves), and the old ones never come back. Dropping every
+    // unknown buffer would stall decoding for good. The surface bounds how
+    // many buffers are out, and the decoder fetches only for a free V4L2
+    // buffer, which it remaps to the new block.
+    switch (mBlockPool->getAllocatorId()) {
+    case C2PlatformAllocatorStore::IGBA:
+    case C2PlatformAllocatorStore::BUFFERQUEUE:
+        return false;
+    default:
+        return true;
+    }
 }
 
 bool VideoFramePool::getVideoFrame(GetVideoFrameCB cb) {
diff --git a/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp b/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp
index 56ab02c..520c3cf 100644
--- a/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp
+++ b/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp
@@ -894,9 +894,21 @@ void V4L2Decoder::onVideoFrameReady(
         outputBuffer = mOutputQueue->getFreeBuffer(v4l2BufferId);
         ALOG_ASSERT(v4l2BufferId == outputBuffer->bufferId());
     } else {
-        // If this happens, this is a bug in VideoFramePool. It should never
-        // provide more blocks than we have V4L2 buffers.
-        ALOGE("Got more different blocks than we have V4L2 buffers for.");
+        // Every V4L2 buffer is mapped already: a surface-backed pool gives
+        // new blocks after an output surface switch. With DMABUF memory any
+        // free V4L2 buffer can take the new block; its old block is not at
+        // the device, so it is simply forgotten.
+        outputBuffer = mOutputQueue->getFreeBuffer();
+        if (outputBuffer) {
+            const size_t v4l2BufferId = outputBuffer->bufferId();
+            for (auto it = mBlockIdToV4L2Id.begin(); it != mBlockIdToV4L2Id.end();) {
+                it = it->second == v4l2BufferId ? mBlockIdToV4L2Id.erase(it) : std::next(it);
+            }
+            mBlockIdToV4L2Id.emplace(blockId, v4l2BufferId);
+            ALOGV("Remapped V4L2 buffer %zu to new block %u", v4l2BufferId, blockId);
+        } else {
+            ALOGE("Got a new block with no free V4L2 buffer for it.");
+        }
     }
 
     if (!outputBuffer) {
